<Step id="STEP_2_SEMANTIC">
      <Goal>Add the layer the tool cannot — meaning.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Consume the artifacts, files, tickets, spec and anchors from `AuditContext` produced by STEP_1
        (`AX_READ_PER_MANIFEST` — the bounded artifacts list + relevant spec anchors, not the whole repo;
        `AX_AUDIT_READS_CODE`, `AX_GIT_DIFF_SCAN`) — do not re-run its producer or git yourself.
        Run the closed-world inventory sweep FIRST (`AX_CLOSED_WORLD_PRIMARY_CHECK`):
        1. collect declared entities from each ticket's `specs/<scope>/<module>/<module>.spec.md` 2 Entity Inventory;
        2. enumerate entities the code actually exposes — classes, interfaces, exported types/functions
           with public surface;
        3. collect the `intro <X> ← <Y>` lines phase agents logged in the Execution Log;
        4. flag every code entity outside inventory ∪ logged-introductions as `CLOSED_WORLD_DRIFT`
           (`BLOCKER`).
        Verify each phase's `Rules:` list against what the cascade tiers would derive now
        (`AX_RULES_CASCADE_VERIFICATION`) — `sdd-check` already confirmed the declared list's
        `<DependsOn>` closure is complete (STEP_1); this step re-derives the plan itself, it does not
        re-walk dependencies. Then judge
        what is irreducibly semantic: does the implementation satisfy the contract's postconditions /
        invariants in substance; is a `real-runtime` claim actually backed or a stub
        (`AX_RUNTIME_BACKING_VERIFICATION`); cross-check every gated `gennady yagni` finding's
        contract label against the round's Execution Log for a matching `yagni <name> ← <reason>`
        line (`AX_AUDIT_YAGNI_CROSSCHECK` — the tool already confirmed the label text and the
        Decision Log liveness in STEP_1, this step only checks the third leg); capture spec gaps as
        insights (`AX_INSIGHT_BACKFLOW_CAPTURE`); judge the operator-facing text as a document
        (`AX_LANGUAGE_QUALITY_CHECK` — flat technical Russian, readable by any engineer level).
        Classify each per `AX_DRIFT_TAXONOMY`, severity per `AX_SEVERITY_TAGGING`.
      </Action>
    </Step>

<Axiom id="AX_READ_PER_MANIFEST">
      When a tool hands a read-manifest for the current step — the exact rule anchors, spec anchors,
      ticket sections, and Target Files to read — read EXACTLY that, nothing beyond it. The manifest's
      "do not read" list and line budget are binding.

      - Pull single blocks by anchor (`sdd-extract <file> <ANCHOR>`), not whole files: reading a fat file
        end-to-end for one section clogs context and degrades a cheaper model on long input.
      - Need something the manifest does not list? That signals the ticket or manifest is wrong — log a
        `discovery` line and surface it; never silently widen the read.
    </Axiom>

<Axiom id="AX_AUDIT_READS_CODE">
      Audit is the only SDD directive allowed to read code. Reading scope is bounded:
      - all files listed in per-phase `Target Files` under ticket Phases;
      - artifacts list passed by orchestrator (union of phase Handoffs);
      - related ports/adapters/services in `Spec References`;
      - files flagged in Execution Log `intro <X> ← <Y>` lines;
      - **all files modified during ticket execution** (see `AX_GIT_DIFF_SCAN`).

      Reading unrelated parts of the project is forbidden — derails focus, consumes context.
    </Axiom>

<Axiom id="AX_CLOSED_WORLD_PRIMARY_CHECK">
      Closed-world drift is the priority check — the main pain audit prevents. The entity world is closed by exactly two sources: the module spec Entity Inventory, and the `intro <X> ← <Y>` lines phase agents logged in the Execution Log. Drift = (public entities in code) ∖ (inventory ∪ logged-introductions); every element of that set is a `CLOSED_WORLD_DRIFT` finding (`BLOCKER`).
    </Axiom>

<Axiom id="AX_RULES_CASCADE_VERIFICATION">
      For each phase block in Phases: that phase's `Rules:` list must equal what scaffolder would derive now from cascade × THAT PHASE's `Target Files` + `kind` — this re-derivation is irreducibly semantic (it needs the current cascade tiers, not just the declared list), so it stays audit's job.

      Cascade tiers (sources of truth):

      | Tier | Source |
      |---|---|
      | traversed-scopes | Cascade Table in `specs/<scope>/<scope>.3-tasks.md` traversed-scope rows |
      | target-scope | `specs/<scope>/<scope>.3-tasks.md` target-scope row + scope spec Architecture/Rules |
      | module | `specs/<scope>/<scope>.3-tasks.md` module row + module spec Module Rules Additions |
      | task | additions appearing as extra entries in a phase's `Rules:` bullet list |

      The transitive `<DependsOn>` closure of the *declared* list is `sdd-check`'s RULES_CASCADE_CLOSURE (`SDD_RULES_CASCADE_UNRESOLVED`, per `AX_MECHANICAL_VIA_SDD_CHECK`) — take that finding as given, do NOT open rule files and walk `<DependsOn>` by hand.

      Plan rows ≠ derived from the cascade tiers → `RULES_CASCADE_MISMATCH` (`MAJOR`, HIGH).
    </Axiom>

<Axiom id="AX_RUNTIME_BACKING_VERIFICATION">
      Runtime Backing and Verification Levels must match reality.
      - Ticket declared `real-runtime` → look for real runtime hooks (event listeners, lifecycle subscribers, OS bridge calls). Only manual publish / test seams → `RUNTIME_BACKING_VIOLATION`.
      - `simulation` allowed only with explicit `Deferred Runtime Scope` either in ticket or in updated spec.
      - `not-implemented` implies real implementation is still ahead (separate task); confirm only contract artifacts exist; no production logic snuck in.
      - For each declared `Verification Level`, corresponding test artifact must exist. Mismatch → `RUNTIME_BACKING_VIOLATION`.
    </Axiom>

<Axiom id="AX_INSIGHT_BACKFLOW_CAPTURE">
      Insights flow back into the spec. If Execution Log or code analysis shows:
      - real complexity of contract turned out higher or lower than spec;
      - new edge cases discovered, not in Requirements & Constraints;
      - spec assumptions turned out incorrect;
      - new dependency emerged, not in Inter-Module Dependencies,

      → `INSIGHT_BACKFLOW` finding with proposed spec-update diff.
    </Axiom>

<Axiom id="AX_LANGUAGE_QUALITY_CHECK">
      Operator-facing text of the audited artifacts (spec Vision / Requirements / Decision Log
      bodies, ticket Purpose / Description, README text) must read as a technical document in flat
      engineering Russian — clear to any engineer on the project, junior developer to architect to
      security engineer. Two layers:
      - **Deterministic pre-filter:** `sdd-check` already flags transliterated anglicisms
        (`SDD_LANGUAGE_CALQUE`, warn) — treat every such warning as a finding to route, do not
        re-judge the word list by hand.
      - **Semantic judgment (this audit)** — style AND comprehension, two different failure modes:
        - *Style:* literary or ornate register, English idioms translated word-for-word, sentences a
          busy colleague would stumble on.
        - *Comprehension* (deeper than style — a sentence can be flawless Russian and still be a
          cipher): an **empty label** — an adjective+noun term with no stated opposite («тонкая
          строка» — thin relative to what? is there a «толстая»?) — is unexplained jargon, not a
          description; a **metaphor stated as fact** («у разговора есть дом») instead of the literal
          mechanism («тред хранится как обычная строка ChatEntity») is rare but dangerous, since it
          passes every other filter; a **term whose meaning lives only in code** must be decoded from
          that code and restated in the sentence, never guessed and never left opaque.
        Test both: read it aloud as a mid-level developer (style); then strip the term from your head
        and ask whether you could still act on the sentence (comprehension) — either failure routes.

      → `LANGUAGE_QUALITY` finding with the concrete sentence and a plain-Russian rewrite proposal.
      Canonical tokens (code, IDs, status tokens, BDD keywords, paths) stay English per
      `AX_OPERATOR_LANGUAGE` and are never flagged.
    </Axiom>

<Axiom id="AX_DRIFT_TAXONOMY">
      Findings are classified by type. Every finding has exactly one:

      | Type | Meaning |
      |---|---|
      | `CLOSED_WORLD_DRIFT` | entity present in code but absent from module spec Entity Inventory AND no `intro` log line |
      | `COMPLETENESS_GAP` | entity / contract / scenario promised in spec but not implemented |
      | `RUNTIME_BACKING_VIOLATION` | declared `real-runtime` actually backed by simulation seam without deferred marker; OR declared `Verification Level` not achievable in test setup |
      | `RULES_COMPLIANCE_VIOLATION` | code violates rules listed in a phase's `Rules:` (any category); OR diff has artefact whose category is unhandled by any active phase |
      | `RULES_CASCADE_MISMATCH` | a phase's `Rules:` list ≠ what scaffolder would derive now from cascade × that phase's `Target Files` + kind |
      | `TASK_ID_DRIFT` | `@tasks` field references non-existent ticket ID (orphan); OR Task-ID duplicated across ticket files (collision); OR `@tasks` field lost prior IDs (regression of append-only) |
      | `BDD_COVERAGE_MISMATCH` | canonical case names in ticket Test Scenario Coverage ≠ real test cases |
      | `EXECUTION_LOG_INCOMPLETE` | mandatory closing lines missing (`ver` / `DONE` per phase, `DONE` at Round close); OR `intro` line absent for an entity that appears in code but not in module Inventory; OR a previous round was edited (round log is append-only); OR `[x]` line with unreplaced `<…>` placeholder (fabricated done) |
      | `INSIGHT_BACKFLOW` | Phase agent discovered something that changes contract / requirement understanding; should flow back into spec |
      | `STALE_AFTER_PIVOT` | scope spec was reworked in `pivot` mode (Pivot Invalidation List exists) but downstream artifacts named in the list have not been refined / reopened / updated |
      | `RULE_FILE_INCOMPLETE` | activated rule file lacks one of the universal checkable sections (`<BeliefState>` / `<AntiPatterns>` / `<VerificationHooks>` / `<RewardCriteria>`) per `AX_RULES_COMPLIANCE_AGAINST_ACTIVATED_RULES`; audit can partially proceed but the gap blocks full coverage |
      | `LANGUAGE_QUALITY` | operator-facing text fails `AX_LANGUAGE_QUALITY_CHECK` — calques (from the `SDD_LANGUAGE_CALQUE` pre-filter) or literary/ornate style the deterministic lint cannot catch |
      | `YAGNI_WAIVER_INCOMPLETE` | a contract `Usage Waiver` label exists for a symbol, but the round's Execution Log has no matching `yagni <name> ← <reason>` line (`AX_AUDIT_YAGNI_CROSSCHECK`) |
      | `ENVIRONMENT_GATE_UNAVAILABLE` | a required gate command (`sdd-verify`, `gennady lint`, `gennady testcov`, or a ticket §5 command) could not execute at all — missing runner, missing script, no network, crashed before producing a pass/fail — as opposed to executing and reporting a red result |
      | `DEAD_CODE_SUSPECT` | an incidental probe exposes apparently dead code outside the current ticket's bounded remediation scope; audit reports it for a separate task and does not widen this run |
    </Axiom>

<Axiom id="AX_SEVERITY_TAGGING">
            Every finding carries a severity:

            | Severity | Meaning |
            |---|---|
            | `BLOCKER` | task cannot be considered complete; merge forbidden |
            | `MAJOR` | behavior, required scope, remediation ownership, or verification is broken or cannot be proved; requires fix; allows merge only with explicit operator acceptance + Decision Log entry |
            | `MINOR` | mechanically fixable documentation, protocol, or style discrepancy that does not make behavior, scope, ownership, or verification ambiguous |
            | `INFO` | observation without mandatory action (often `INSIGHT_BACKFLOW`) |

            Confidence constrains what severity may decide. `HIGH` and `MEDIUM` may participate in the
            verdict table. `LOW` is an unconfirmed observation: before summary, run one bounded independent
            check that can raise it to `MEDIUM`/`HIGH`; if it remains `LOW`, emit it only as `INFO`. A
            `LOW` finding never causes `FAIL`, opens an Execution Round, or authorizes an artifact change.
            It remains visible to the operator instead of becoming an interactive halt.

            Severity does not depend on agent's mood or on whether the mismatch is in code or prose.
            Closed-world drift = `BLOCKER` by default; a coding-rules violation that can change behavior or
            verification = `MAJOR`; stylistic deviation = `MINOR`. A protocol or documentation mismatch is
            `MAJOR` only when it makes required behavior, scope, remediation ownership, or verification
            ambiguous or unprovable. Otherwise it is `MINOR` and cannot force another execution round.
            Distinguish that from new knowledge: a sound execution choice that preserves current
            requirements but suggests enriching canonical text is `INSIGHT_BACKFLOW` `INFO`, presented for
            optional operator review. A known canonical fact copied incorrectly into another artifact is
            documentation mismatch and remains `MINOR`.
            A claimed verification result with no executed evidence is not paper drift: it leaves required
            behavior unproved and is `MAJOR`.

            **Overall status is computed from severities, never judged.** First matching row wins; print which row matched:

            | Condition | Status |
            |---|---|
            | any open `BLOCKER` | `FAIL` |
            | any open `MAJOR` without operator acknowledgement | `FAIL` |
            | every open `MAJOR` acknowledged + Decision Log entry, and no `BLOCKER` | `PASS_WITH_ACKNOWLEDGED_RISKS` |
            | only `MINOR` / `INFO`, or nothing | `PASS` |

            `RULE_FILE_INCOMPLETE` and every other project-scope finding enters this table capped at `MINOR` (per `AX_FINDING_ROUTING`): shared infrastructure no task owns cannot decide that task's verdict. The `sev` field still reports the finding's own severity — the cap applies to the computation, not to the record.

